---
/**
 * The wallet check.
 *
 * A trader's question is not "how vulnerable is the market" but "how vulnerable am I"
 * (product/CUSTOMERS.md). The lookup runs in the visitor's own browser against Hyperliquid's
 * public endpoint -- no server, no key, nothing stored.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import { botHandle, liveExample, url } from '../lib/data';

const example = liveExample();
const bot = botHandle();
---

<Base
  title="Check your liquidation risk — Hyperliquid"
  description="Paste a Hyperliquid address. See how close it is to liquidation, and whether it has anything left to defend with. Free, no signup."
>
  <h1>How close are you?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Paste any Hyperliquid address. We read its positions straight from the exchange and tell you
    the one thing most tools leave out — whether it has anything left to defend with.
  </p>

  <form id="f" class="checkform" autocomplete="off">
    <input id="addr" type="text" spellcheck="false" placeholder="0x…" aria-label="Hyperliquid address" />
    <button type="submit">Check</button>
  </form>
  <p class="hint">
    No signup, nothing stored. The lookup happens in your browser, straight to Hyperliquid.
    {example && <button class="linkish" id="demo" type="button" data-address={example}>Try an example</button>}
  </p>

  <div id="out"></div>

  {/* The check is where someone learns they are exposed. That is the only moment the alert is an
      obvious thing to want, so the offer belongs after the answer -- never before it. */}
  {bot && (
    <section id="alertcta" hidden>
      <Note title="Want to be told when this changes?">
        The bot messages you when this address crosses into a tighter band, or loses the collateral
        to defend itself — and says nothing at all otherwise.
        <a href={url('alerts.html')}>Set it up →</a>
      </Note>
    </section>
  )}

  <section id="explain" hidden>
    <h2>Why free collateral is the number that matters</h2>
    <p>
      When price moves against a leveraged position, the usual escape is to add margin and push the
      liquidation price away. Whether that is possible depends on <em>free collateral</em> — and the
      obvious way to work it out is wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      We compared the arithmetic against the exchange's own figure on 74 wallets. It matched
      <strong>19%</strong> of the time, and misclassified <strong>one wallet in five</strong> as able
      to defend when it was not. Always in the direction of looking safer.
      <a href={url('research/free-collateral-misclassification.html')}>The measurement →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/check';
</script>
